    "body": "No tracking issue yet; opening as a draft to discuss the design with a working implementation.\n\nFollowing [Working with us](https://github.com/cli/cli/blob/trunk/docs/working-with-us.md#step-0-create-an-extension), the same behavior is available today as an extension: [mrecachinas/gh-verify-download](https://github.com/mrecachinas/gh-verify-download) (`gh extension install mrecachinas/gh-verify-download`).\n\n### Description\n\nBefore running a downloaded release asset, it's worth knowing whether the release is immutable and how the asset was built. `gh` can check this today, but only after downloading and across several commands, so people rarely do.\n\n`gh release download --verify` runs these checks **before** downloading and then asks whether to continue:\n\n- **Immutable releases:** GitHub's signed release attestation must verify, match this release, and list each asset's name and digest.\n- **Build provenance:** each asset's SLSA attestation must be signed by GitHub Actions and built from the release's repository. The workflow, commit, ref, and run are shown.\n- **After downloading**, each file must match its expected sha256.\n\nIn a terminal the prompt defaults to *No* if anything failed; non-interactive runs fail instead. A release asset URL can also be passed in place of a tag.\n\n### How did you test this change?\n\nI built this branch and ran it against public releases.\n\nAn immutable, attested release downloads after showing its provenance:\n\n```\n$ gh release download --verify https://github.com/cli/cli/releases/download/v2.102.1/gh_2.102.1_macOS_arm64.zip\ncli/cli v2.102.1\n\u2713 Immutable release, verified by GitHub's signed release attestation\n\ngh_2.102.1_macOS_arm64.zip\n\u2713 Listed in the signed release attestation\n\u2713 Verified SLSA build provenance\n  - Build instructions: https://github.com/cli/cli/blob/89d7dcec017194af1d6f8853176b2dae9c1da762/.github/workflows/deployment.yml\n  - Source:             https://github.com/cli/cli/tree/89d7dcec017194af1d6f8853176b2dae9c1da762\n  - Source ref:         refs/heads/bagtoad/go-1-27-3-patch-on-v2-102-0\n  - Build run:          https://github.com/cli/cli/actions/runs/39482998765/attempts/1\n  - Runner:             github-hosted\n```\n\nA mutable release without attestations is refused (exit 1, nothing written). In a terminal I instead saw `? Download mockery_2.53.8_Darwin_arm64.tar.gz? (y/N)`.\n\n```\n$ gh release download --verify https://github.com/vektra/mockery/releases/download/v2.53.8/mockery_2.53.8_Darwin_arm64.tar.gz\nvektra/mockery v2.53.8\n! Not an immutable release; assets can be replaced after publishing\n\nmockery_2.53.8_Darwin_arm64.tar.gz\nX No build provenance attestation found\n\nrefusing to download: 1 asset failed verification; run without `--verify` to download anyway\n```\n\nI also saw that `-O -` writes the verified bytes (piping to `shasum -a 256` gave the attested `08bfef98\u2026aa2aeb`), and that GHES hosts and `--archive` are rejected up front with an explanation.\n\n### Key points\n\n- Assets are downloaded to a private temp directory and checked there; they're only written to the destination (or stdout) if every asset passes. A failed check leaves existing files untouched.\n- Provenance may be signed by any Actions workflow on the host (so reusable workflows work), but the source repository is pinned, matching `gh attestation verify --repo`.\n- Mutable releases only warn, since they have no release attestation; build provenance decides.\n- Without `--verify`, an asset literally named `-` is now saved as a file rather than streamed to stdout.\n\n### Notes for reviewers\n\nStart with `pkg/cmd/release/download/provenance.go` (`confirmProvenance`, `check`, `checkRelease`), then `downloadVerified` in `download.go`. It reuses the existing attestation client, Sigstore verifier, and `shared.AttestationVerifier`.\n\nOpen questions: should this be a flag or its own command, and should mutable releases with valid provenance pass silently in scripts?\n\n### Authorship and follow-up\n\nWho wrote this:\n\n- [ ] A human wrote it.\n- [x] An agent wrote it under close human direction.\n- [ ] An agent wrote it independently, and no human has guided the implementation beyond the initial prompt.\n\nWho answers review comments:\n\n- [x] @mrecachinas will read and reply directly.\n- [ ] An agent will draft replies and @username will read them before they are posted.\n- [ ] Nobody has explicitly committed to replying.\n",
